{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ESCAPE HATCH — required, per operator direction. The goal is NO AD-HOC raw git, not 'no raw\ngit ever'. Tonight is the proof: the store was wedged by the stash race, every rebar write\nfailed, and raw git in the tracker was the ONLY way out. A hard prohibition with no sanctioned\ndoor would have left the operator stranded with a dead ticket system.\n\nSo the design must ship BOTH halves:\n\n(a) THE SUPPORTED DOOR — a first-class maintenance surface that performs the raw-git\n    operations correctly, so nobody has to improvise them:\n      - it is the ONLY blessed way to mutate the tracker outside the normal event-append path;\n      - it TAKES A BACKUP REF BEFORE THE FIRST WRITE, not mid-run. (The 'pre-a3-remediation'\n        tag was stamped after 2 of 4 batches and was therefore useless as a rollback point —\n        that is exactly the mistake to design out.)\n      - it REFUSES when unpushed local ticket commits would be lost (`rev-list\n        origin/tickets..HEAD` non-empty), because that is the one condition that separates a\n        recoverable local mess from actual event loss;\n      - it is AUDITABLE: what ran, when, by whom, and what changed — recorded durably rather\n        than living only in a shell history.\n\n(b) THE BREAK-GLASS — an explicit, deliberately-inconvenient flag (`--force`-style, requiring\n    a written reason) for the genuinely unforeseen case the supported door does not cover. It\n    should be loud in the output and recorded, so a reviewer can later see it was used and why.\n    Same posture AGENTS.md already takes for the claim/close gates: an escape hatch for a human\n    operator's judgment call, not a routine agent move.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT: a reason to keep the current situation, where agents reach for `git add -A`\n/ `commit` / `merge` / `push` / `rm` in the tracker because nothing better exists. That is\nwhat happened in this session's remediation, and it is how source files and stray state get\ncommitted to the tickets branch.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO THE AUTO-HEAL AC: auto-heal removes MOST of the need for the hatch — tonight's\nwedge satisfied the safe auto-heal condition (no unpushed commits) and would have self-repaired\nwith no human intervention at all. The hatch exists for what auto-heal deliberately refuses to\ntouch, i.e. the cases where events would be lost and a human must decide.\n\nAdd to the acceptance criteria:\n- [ ] A supported maintenance entrypoint exists with backup-before-first-write, a refusal when\n      unpushed ticket commits are present, and a durable audit record.\n- [ ] A break-glass override exists for cases the supported entrypoint does not cover; it\n      requires an explicit written reason and is surfaced loudly in output.\n- [ ] Docs state the rule as 'no AD-HOC raw git in the tracker; use the maintenance entrypoint',\n      and name the break-glass, so the guidance is actionable rather than a bare prohibition."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786329632057794001,"uuid":"6c8d8820-5804-40e5-afeb-30f2a2bc6651"}